Mail, and the token that verifies an address
============================================

Phase 1 of doc/proposals/007-identidad.md, the piece that was left for
when it was needed: an account is registered to an address, and until
somebody reads a message sent to that address, the address is a claim.

Two things live here, and they are not the same thing.

**Verifying an address is the core's.**  It is a proof, like a password
is a proof: what it decides is whether a client may claim an identity,
and that decision has to be the same on every server of the network.
include/mail.h and ircd/mail.c hold it, and ircd/m_account.c holds the
half that needs a client.

**Delivering the mail is a module's.**  SMTP has a server at the other
end, a configuration, a queue and a set of failure modes that have
nothing to do with IRC -- and there is more than one way to do it: hand
it to the local MTA, speak SMTP to a relay, call somebody's HTTP API.  So
the core holds the `Mail{}` block and the messages in flight and
dispatches to one registered provider, which is the arrangement db.h and
cache.h have.  `modules/workers/sendmail/` is the one that ships.


THE TOKEN
=========

Signed, not stored.  A token carries what it asserts -- an address and an
expiry -- and an HMAC over it:

  1.<base64 of "expiry:address">.<base64 of 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA-256>

The key is derived from the `Security{}` key with HMAC and a label of its
own, so it is not the key that ciphers hostnames and cannot be used as
one.  Every server of a network has that key by construction
(doc/readme.accounting), which is what makes a token minted on one server
recognisable on another -- and what makes "nothing is written down" work:
there is no table of outstanding tokens to keep, to expire, or to leak.

The cost of not storing them is that a token cannot be revoked before it
expires.  That is why the window is short (`verify_window`, a day by
default) and why what a token grants is one thing only: the address is
marked verified.  It is not a login, it does not grant `+r`, and it
cannot be replayed into one.

Sixteen bytes of HMAC and not thirty-two because a person pastes this
back, and 128 bits is far past what forging one is worth.


WHAT A USER DOES
================

  /msg NickServ REGISTER maria@example.net secreto

registers the nickname and, if this server can send mail, posts a
message to the address.  The message says what to send back:

  ACCOUNT VERIFY 1.MTc4OTY1NTIwNDpt...==.j4TQgGhY+ycIX0MBXWw9qA==

or, with `verify_url` set in the `Mail{}` block, a link that carries the
same token to whatever serves that link.

  ACCOUNT VERIFY

with no token asks for another message, to the address this client
authenticated with.  There is no form of this command that takes an
address: that would be a way to have the server post a message to
anybody, signed by the network.  NickServ offers the same two things --
`VERIFY` and `VERIFY <token>` -- in its own words, and they come back
through the same function, so there is one path from a token to a
verified address.

The answers are numerics, because the proof is the core's:

  988  RPL_VERIFYSENT   a message is on its way to <address>
  989  RPL_VERIFIED     <address> is verified
  983  ERR_ACCOUNTFAIL  why not
  986  ERR_NOTAUTHENTICATED  you have no address for this to be sent to

A token that is not one and a token from another network get the same
answer, deliberately: telling them apart tells whoever is trying which
half they got right.  An expired token says so, because that one is
useful -- the answer is "ask for another".


CONFIGURATION
=============

  Mail {
    from = "noreply@example.net";
    program = "/usr/sbin/sendmail";
    timeout = 30 seconds;
    verify_window = 1 days;
    resend_interval = 5 minutes;
    # verify_url = "https://example.net/verify?t=%s";
  };

`from` is the only required field, and the core applies it to every
message: a provider cannot forget it and two providers cannot disagree
about it.  `program` is read by the sendmail module and not by the core,
exactly as `Redis{}`'s host is read by the redis driver.

`timeout` is how long the server waits for the provider to say whether it
sent the message, clamped to two minutes.  Nothing waits for *delivery*:
a client is told the message was accepted, which is the only thing the
server can honestly say without a mail server and a person reading their
inbox.

`resend_interval` is the shortest gap between two messages to one
connection.  It is per connection and not per account because what is
being rationed is this server mailing somebody, and the client asking has
not necessarily proved anything about the address yet.

`verify_url` must contain `%s`, where the token goes.  Without it the
message carries the command to send back, which is what a network with no
web side wants.

With no `Mail{}` block, `mail_available()` is false, nothing is sent, and
NickServ does not offer.  A server that sends no mail is an ordinary
server.


THE SENDMAIL MODULE
===================

  Module { name = "sendmail"; };

The default because it needs nothing: every host that sends mail at all
has a `sendmail`, its interface has not changed in forty years, and the
queue, the retries, the TLS and the reputation are somebody else's
problem -- which is where they belong.

**The program runs on a worker thread**, never on the main thread: fork,
exec, write and wait all block, and an MTA that takes a second to accept
a message would be a second in which the server did nothing at all.  What
crosses to the worker is bytes -- the composed message and the program to
run -- and what comes back is an exit status, which is the rule
doc/readme.workers states.  With `FEAT_WORKER_THREADS` at zero the module
says so and refuses, the way the identity module refuses to hash a
password.

The message is composed by the module, because the format is the one that
program expects: headers and a body on stdin, `-t` to read the recipient
from the headers, `-i` so a lone dot does not end it early, and `-f` for
the envelope sender.  The core has already refused anything with a
newline in the address or the subject, which is what would otherwise turn
one of those lines into several.

A module that speaks SMTP to a relay registers the same way and the core
does not care which is loaded.  It will want fields in the `Mail{}` block
that are not there yet -- a host, a port, credentials -- and it can have
them when it exists: configuration nobody reads is configuration invented
blind.


WHAT THIS IS NOT
================

- It is **not a login**.  A verified address says the address is real.
  What grants `+r` is a credential, and that is doc/readme.sasl.
- It is **not a queue**.  A message that the provider could not send is
  logged and dropped; the user asks again.  Retrying belongs to the MTA,
  which has spent forty years learning how.
- It does **not cross a link**.  Every server checks the same signature
  against the same key and asks the same provider, so there is nothing to
  route -- the same reason AUTHENTICATE and ACCOUNT have no P10 token.


WHERE TO LOOK
=============

  include/mail.h            the register, the token, the block
  ircd/mail.c               all of it that needs no client
  ircd/m_account.c          ACCOUNT VERIFY, and the message composed
  ircd/test/mail_t.c        the token, one changed character at a time
  modules/workers/sendmail/ the provider that ships
  modules/services/irc_services/svc_nickserv.c   VERIFY, in the bot's words
  tests/identity_db/        the end-to-end test, with a stand-in MTA
